1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,140
How do we construct natural intermediate
orientations between two poses?

2
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:08,880
We will observe driving and flight,

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:12,800
then visualize the short path and speed of
quaternion interpolation.

4
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:16,560
Next, we will convert Euler angles,
axis-angle,

5
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:18,680
quaternions and matrices under one convention.

6
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:22,000
Finally, we will calculate the midpoint of a

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,100
ninety-degree turn and verify a conversion
round trip.

8
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:29,230
This is driving on a bend in Forza Horizon4.

9
00:00:29,550 --> 00:00:32,470
Read the car facing separately from the
following camera.

10
00:00:35,798 --> 00:00:38,398
Matching the first and last directions does
not

11
00:00:38,398 --> 00:00:40,498
prevent an awkward sudden turn between them.

12
00:00:43,666 --> 00:00:47,746
Compare the front of the car, the bend and the
moving background.

13
00:00:51,014 --> 00:00:54,414
The footage does not reveal which
interpolation function the game uses.

14
00:00:54,834 --> 00:00:57,554
It shows why intermediate orientations matter.

15
00:01:00,842 --> 00:01:04,902
Changes in apparent car size also involve
camera and distance.

16
00:01:04,982 --> 00:01:10,862
Following its facing separately helps avoid
mixing translation with rotation.

17
00:01:13,990 --> 00:01:18,110
Our next diagram will hold position and camera
fixed and change only orientation.

18
00:01:18,970 --> 00:01:23,610
Consider the midpoint between identity and
a180-degree turn about z.

19
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:29,350
Averaging the entries yields a diagonal
of0,0,1. Two axes collapse.

20
00:01:29,570 --> 00:01:32,050
Quaternions must maintain unit length too.

21
00:01:32,157 --> 00:01:35,730
A component blend generally leaves the unit
surface.

22
00:01:35,970 --> 00:01:41,530
We will distinguish a normalized linear blend
from following an arc on the unit sphere.

23
00:01:41,810 --> 00:01:44,410
Matrices can be interpolated with methods that
respect the rotation structure;

24
00:01:44,450 --> 00:01:47,370
naive entry blending is the problem.

25
00:01:48,017 --> 00:01:52,417
As we learned, q and minus q represent the
same orientation,

26
00:01:52,737 --> 00:01:55,097
yet a component path between them can differ.

27
00:01:55,437 --> 00:01:58,777
Take the dot product of unit endpoints. If it
is negative,

28
00:01:59,057 --> 00:02:01,977
flip every component of the end quaternion.

29
00:02:02,257 --> 00:02:07,797
This selects the closer representative of the
same endpoint; it is not conjugation.

30
00:02:08,057 --> 00:02:12,177
For endpoints q and minus q,

31
00:02:12,497 --> 00:02:15,057
sign alignment makes them identical, avoiding
a zero midpoint.

32
00:02:15,057 --> 00:02:20,077
A physical180-degree separation has two
equally short paths.

33
00:02:20,397 --> 00:02:22,757
Choose a direction with an explicit policy.

34
00:02:22,957 --> 00:02:25,237
We can also use the relative rotation from the
previous lesson.

35
00:02:25,557 --> 00:02:29,237
After sign alignment, delta is the end
quaternion times the inverse of the start.

36
00:02:29,537 --> 00:02:33,437
Applying delta to the power t to the start
follows the same short arc.

37
00:02:34,067 --> 00:02:38,827
Track the car facing as it follows the next
bend.

38
00:02:41,679 --> 00:02:44,359
An endpoint image alone does not tell whether
the

39
00:02:44,359 --> 00:02:49,651
turn was short or included an extra
revolution.

40
00:02:49,911 --> 00:02:53,851
Interpolation between two orientations chooses
a path

41
00:02:54,211 --> 00:02:57,371
without storing the number of earlier
revolutions.

42
00:03:00,183 --> 00:03:08,195
Interpolating a position along the road
differs from interpolating orientation.

43
00:03:08,195 --> 00:03:14,715
Even when two moments have similar facing, the
intervening turn requires observing

44
00:03:15,175 --> 00:03:18,035
the motion. Distinguish endpoint orientation
from rotation history over time.

45
00:03:21,147 --> 00:03:25,027
Now we will relate an arc on the unit sphere
to the orientation change.

46
00:03:26,067 --> 00:03:29,227
Unit quaternions lie on a sphere in four
dimensions,

47
00:03:29,232 --> 00:03:31,407
with squared components summing to one.

48
00:03:31,627 --> 00:03:34,507
This circle illustrates the relevant arc in a
two-dimensional section,

49
00:03:34,787 --> 00:03:37,867
rather than depicting the full
four-dimensional sphere.

50
00:03:38,037 --> 00:03:43,547
Let d be the sign-aligned dot product, and let
alpha be its arccosine.

51
00:03:43,727 --> 00:03:48,327
The quaternion arc angle differs from the
physical turn

52
00:03:48,587 --> 00:03:51,407
angle: the minimum physical rotation is twice
alpha.

53
00:03:51,407 --> 00:03:56,447
Sine-ratio weights combine the endpoints into
the point at fraction t of the arc.

54
00:03:56,887 --> 00:04:01,447
This is spherical linear interpolation. It
does not mean the components change linearly.

55
00:04:02,083 --> 00:04:06,403
Our code requires finite t between zero and
one.

56
00:04:06,943 --> 00:04:09,543
Validate and normalize both quaternion
endpoints first.

57
00:04:09,693 --> 00:04:17,203
Near-zero or nonfinite inputs need an error or
an explicit fallback to a valid orientation.

58
00:04:17,443 --> 00:04:19,743
Flip the endpoint when the dot is negative,

59
00:04:19,743 --> 00:04:24,043
then clamp the dot to protect arccosine from
rounding error.

60
00:04:24,183 --> 00:04:26,623
When d exceeds0.9995, this example uses
normalized

61
00:04:26,883 --> 00:04:31,123
linear interpolation to avoid a small sine
denominator.

62
00:04:31,123 --> 00:04:33,943
Otherwise, use the sine weights and normalize
the result.

63
00:04:34,403 --> 00:04:37,143
Check the endpoint orientations at t zero and
one.

64
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:42,200
In these other driving intervals, separate car
facing from background motion.

65
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:48,980
The edit between excerpts is not a computed
intermediate pose.

66
00:04:49,180 --> 00:04:52,540
Observe continuous motion within each excerpt.

67
00:04:54,980 --> 00:05:02,540
Code produces intermediate rotations by
changing t between the endpoints.

68
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:07,960
Imagine both a forward line and an up line on
the car.

69
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:14,120
A single direction can miss its twist.

70
00:05:14,120 --> 00:05:18,040
A cut can change location and camera. Observe
continuous pose changes within

71
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:23,700
each excerpt before connecting them to the
next fixed-condition calculation.

72
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:31,860
The next calculation holds camera and position
fixed. For a ninety-degree turn,

73
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:35,160
examine t equal to zero point two five and
zero point five.

74
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:38,660
Start at[1,0,0,0], ending at a z90-degree

75
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:44,580
turn:[cos45degrees,0,0,sin45degrees].

76
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:47,260
The dot is square root two over two: alpha
is45degrees,

77
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:51,260
while the physical rotation is90degrees.

78
00:05:51,335 --> 00:05:55,040
At t equal to zero point two five,

79
00:05:55,540 --> 00:05:57,960
the object rotates twenty-two point five
degrees, the first of four equal steps.

80
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:01,540
At the halfway value t equal to zero point
five, it rotates forty-five degrees.

81
00:06:01,602 --> 00:06:05,760
At the third step, t equal to zero point seven
five, it is sixty-seven point five degrees.

82
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:08,980
Equal t intervals give equal rotation-angle
intervals.

83
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:13,100
Each quaternion is converted to a matrix to
draw the body axes.

84
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:16,280
We can verify intermediate results as well as
endpoints.

85
00:06:17,040 --> 00:06:20,540
NLERP aligns signs, blends components linearly
and

86
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:23,000
normalizes: a simple calculation that
preserves unit length.

87
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:27,620
Linear progression of t generally does not
produce constant physical angular speed.

88
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:29,780
Between identity and a120-degree turn,

89
00:06:29,793 --> 00:06:33,200
NLERP at one quarter rotates
approximately27.8degrees.

90
00:06:33,235 --> 00:06:35,740
SLERP gives30degrees there. Both give60degrees
halfway,

91
00:06:35,940 --> 00:06:39,920
but differ at other points along the path.

92
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:44,280
Small differences may be hard to see.

93
00:06:44,425 --> 00:06:47,200
Compare motion requirements and cost without
assuming SLERP is always faster.

94
00:06:47,833 --> 00:06:52,833
Driving speed and angular speed are separate
quantities.

95
00:06:54,617 --> 00:06:59,617
Observe distance travelled and change in car
facing separately.

96
00:07:01,421 --> 00:07:06,541
The camera also moves, so this footage is not
a measurement of exact angular velocity.

97
00:07:06,841 --> 00:07:10,885
The speedometer reports travel speed, not the
angular velocity of orientation.

98
00:07:10,885 --> 00:07:18,549
Keep units and measured quantities distinct.

99
00:07:18,869 --> 00:07:22,789
A large camera turn does not imply the same
car rotation.

100
00:07:22,789 --> 00:07:28,653
Observe car and road together to separate pose
changes.

101
00:07:28,893 --> 00:07:33,453
Our fixed-axis calculation can compare turn
angles over equal time intervals.

102
00:07:33,733 --> 00:07:36,213
Changing the time function changes the speed.

103
00:07:36,945 --> 00:07:44,013
Constant angular speed requires fixed
endpoints and t that is linear in time.

104
00:07:44,293 --> 00:07:50,013
An easing function follows the same arc with a
varying speed.

105
00:07:50,145 --> 00:07:53,153
Repeatedly interpolating from the current pose
toward a

106
00:07:53,153 --> 00:07:56,933
target differs from one fixed-endpoint
interpolation.

107
00:07:57,233 --> 00:08:01,253
A fixed fraction per frame may yield different
responses at different frame rates.

108
00:08:01,613 --> 00:08:03,933
Design the fraction using elapsed time.

109
00:08:03,933 --> 00:08:08,273
Moving-target tracking and long animation
curves require their own design;

110
00:08:08,713 --> 00:08:10,953
a single SLERP does not solve everything.

111
00:08:12,107 --> 00:08:14,687
Making w positive independently each frame can
create

112
00:08:14,687 --> 00:08:17,287
a component sign jump at the180-degree
boundary.

113
00:08:17,527 --> 00:08:20,847
Flipping a new quaternion when its dot with
the previous

114
00:08:20,847 --> 00:08:24,067
one is negative can maintain a nearby
representative.

115
00:08:24,407 --> 00:08:29,047
That choice helps component continuity, but
does not recover the number of revolutions.

116
00:08:29,327 --> 00:08:32,007
If an animation intentionally spins a full
turn,

117
00:08:32,007 --> 00:08:34,687
preserve intermediate keys or accumulated turn
information.

118
00:08:34,907 --> 00:08:38,267
The desired direction at180degrees is a design

119
00:08:38,367 --> 00:08:41,747
policy: shortest alone does not make the
choice unique.

120
00:08:42,367 --> 00:08:46,267
This is wingsuit flight down a mountain in
Riders Republic.

121
00:08:46,407 --> 00:08:51,820
Observe the bank of the arms and body.

122
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:56,420
Position continues changing while facing and
up directions change too.

123
00:08:56,680 --> 00:09:03,833
One facing arrow does not capture the whole
orientation.

124
00:09:04,133 --> 00:09:12,207
Storing the arrival orientation does not
preserve every rotation made along the flight.

125
00:09:12,547 --> 00:09:15,207
Separate the following camera motion too.

126
00:09:15,407 --> 00:09:19,107
This footage does not expose internal
quaternion or interpolation code.

127
00:09:19,107 --> 00:09:27,320
With different equipment too, distinguish
torso facing from body bank.

128
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:31,020
Attached forward and up directions provide a
frame for reading orientation.

129
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:38,633
Do not connect cuts or rewind screens into
continuous motion. We selected actual

130
00:09:38,833 --> 00:09:47,087
action within separate excerpts; imagine the
three attached axes in each.

131
00:09:47,427 --> 00:09:51,747
We will now choose a suitable representation
for each task and convert between them.

132
00:09:52,367 --> 00:09:55,667
Euler angles suit human reading and editing,
with order,

133
00:09:55,847 --> 00:09:59,367
equivalent encodings and singular poses to
handle.

134
00:09:59,607 --> 00:10:01,907
Quaternions suit composition and
interpolation,

135
00:10:01,982 --> 00:10:06,127
but need unit-length and sign management and
are hard to edit directly.

136
00:10:06,387 --> 00:10:10,887
Matrices expose the three axes and suit
transforming many vectors.

137
00:10:11,147 --> 00:10:13,507
Check orthonormality and determinant.

138
00:10:13,767 --> 00:10:18,487
Float32 components alone occupy12bytes for
Euler,16for a

139
00:10:18,487 --> 00:10:23,487
quaternion and36for a3-by-3matrix, excluding
padding and metadata.

140
00:10:23,987 --> 00:10:26,827
Axis-angle explains a single turn clearly.
Measure compression and runtime in your

141
00:10:27,147 --> 00:10:33,387
implementation; other representations also
have rotation-aware interpolation methods.

142
00:10:33,707 --> 00:10:36,347
Storage or transmission may compress
components.

143
00:10:36,907 --> 00:10:41,687
Reconstructing an omitted component from unit
length requires sign rules

144
00:10:41,792 --> 00:10:44,927
and quantization-error checks. Sixteen bytes
is not a universal minimum.

145
00:10:45,850 --> 00:10:51,450
All conversions preserve our right-handed
column-vector local-to-world convention.

146
00:10:51,750 --> 00:10:58,690
Use Hamilton quaternions in wxyz order and
radians for trigonometric inputs.

147
00:10:58,890 --> 00:11:07,470
Euler uses moving axes Y-X-Z, with matrix
product Ry times Rx times Rz.

148
00:11:07,630 --> 00:11:12,910
Mixing the book's left-handed row-vector
formulas can change signs and order.

149
00:11:13,190 --> 00:11:15,830
Memory layout is a separate issue.

150
00:11:15,850 --> 00:11:19,930
Euler and quaternions connect bidirectionally
through matrices;

151
00:11:19,930 --> 00:11:23,270
axis-angle connects to quaternions through the
half-angle formula.

152
00:11:23,900 --> 00:11:33,088
Watch the body bank and turn during flight.
Distinguish screen up from body up.

153
00:11:33,368 --> 00:11:36,908
Equipment changes across edits between these
excerpts;

154
00:11:37,188 --> 00:11:43,096
those cuts are not one continuous rotation.

155
00:11:43,316 --> 00:11:49,836
Within each excerpt, observe the attached body
axes and the following camera.

156
00:11:53,564 --> 00:11:58,044
Changing how attached axes are represented
must not rotate the object.

157
00:11:58,664 --> 00:12:05,612
Distinguish converting a representation from
applying a new rotation.

158
00:12:05,612 --> 00:12:10,132
Equivalent pose does not require identical
components.

159
00:12:10,492 --> 00:12:14,732
Compare the three resulting axes in the same
coordinate frame.

160
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:23,240
A matrix and a quaternion should describe the
same pose in the same frame.

161
00:12:23,560 --> 00:12:25,680
Our next diagram calculates that relationship.

162
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:33,520
Build Y, X and Z matrices from heading, pitch
and bank, then multiply in our declared order.

163
00:12:33,820 --> 00:12:41,120
For extraction, indices start at zero. Sine of
pitch is minus the entry at row1,column2.

164
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:46,260
Away from singular poses, heading is atan2 of
R02 and R22;

165
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:49,620
bank is atan2 of R10 and R11.

166
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:51,640
This implementation extracts pitch with atan2
and uses the

167
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:55,060
hypotenuse of two matrix entries to detect a
singular pose.

168
00:12:55,060 --> 00:12:58,600
At pitch plus or minus90degrees,

169
00:12:59,140 --> 00:13:02,520
choose bank zero and heading atan2 of minus
R20 and R00.

170
00:13:03,080 --> 00:13:06,960
Different returned Euler values can represent
the same orientation.

171
00:13:07,400 --> 00:13:09,260
Compare the reconstructed matrices in a round
trip.

172
00:13:10,117 --> 00:13:15,977
With a quaternion at unit length, its four
components give the nine

173
00:13:16,297 --> 00:13:19,337
rotation-matrix entries. Check and normalize a
general-length input first.

174
00:13:19,737 --> 00:13:24,177
Insert a quaternion for a ninety-degree z
turn.

175
00:13:24,717 --> 00:13:28,697
The first matrix row is zero, minus one, zero;
the second is one, zero,

176
00:13:28,937 --> 00:13:32,817
zero; the third is zero, zero, one. This
matches the earlier matrix.

177
00:13:33,057 --> 00:13:41,057
The first column gives local x in world
coordinates:[0,1,0]; the second is[-1,0,0].

178
00:13:41,537 --> 00:13:49,117
Multiplying[1,0,0]gives[0,1,0], matching the
two-sided quaternion rotation.

179
00:13:49,617 --> 00:13:53,797
If signs differ, check axes, rotation
direction and input/output spaces first.

180
00:13:53,797 --> 00:13:58,037
The full formula is also preserved in the
reproducible code.

181
00:13:58,733 --> 00:14:03,453
Look at the car again. Follow the body facing
and road inclination.

182
00:14:03,713 --> 00:14:08,133
We can record the same visible orientation as
a matrix or a quaternion.

183
00:14:09,133 --> 00:14:12,813
Changing representation does not automatically
change position or speed,

184
00:14:13,093 --> 00:14:15,633
and must preserve the input/output frames.

185
00:14:16,593 --> 00:14:20,673
We have not read stored values from the game.

186
00:14:20,873 --> 00:14:24,693
The footage prepares us to check equivalent
axes in our calculation.

187
00:14:25,813 --> 00:14:30,753
Matching forward directions alone can still
leave different up directions.

188
00:14:30,753 --> 00:14:34,473
Follow the bank as well as facing.

189
00:14:35,493 --> 00:14:39,413
Mathematically we can transform basis axes
using stored rotations. Instead of

190
00:14:39,633 --> 00:14:44,933
inferring hidden values from pixels, we verify
them in the next independent calculation.

191
00:14:46,013 --> 00:14:50,113
We will check that conversion remains valid
near a half-turn.

192
00:14:50,733 --> 00:14:55,153
First require an orthonormal matrix with
determinant plus one.

193
00:14:55,493 --> 00:14:58,093
Do not interpret scale or reflection as a
rotation.

194
00:14:58,253 --> 00:15:00,453
The trace is the diagonal sum.

195
00:15:00,553 --> 00:15:04,573
A positive trace suits the branch that
calculates a large w component.

196
00:15:04,813 --> 00:15:10,873
At a180-degree rotation w is zero, so using
only a formula that divides by w is unsafe.

197
00:15:11,093 --> 00:15:14,373
Otherwise choose the x,y or z branch
associated with the largest diagonal entry,

198
00:15:14,373 --> 00:15:18,613
avoiding division by a small component.

199
00:15:18,613 --> 00:15:22,553
For a z180-degree turn the z branch
yields[0,0,0,1];

200
00:15:22,553 --> 00:15:25,753
the opposite sign represents the same pose.

201
00:15:26,313 --> 00:15:29,593
Normalize the result, rebuild its matrix and
compare with the input.

202
00:15:29,853 --> 00:15:32,933
Quaternion normalization is not an arbitrary
repair of an invalid matrix.

203
00:15:33,666 --> 00:15:37,506
Convert heading about Y, pitch about X and
bank

204
00:15:37,606 --> 00:15:42,046
about Z into individual half-angle
quaternions.

205
00:15:42,266 --> 00:15:49,286
Under our convention, multiply qy times qx
times qz, matching the matrix product.

206
00:15:49,526 --> 00:15:51,926
For the reverse, build the unit-quaternion
matrix and use our Euler extraction.

207
00:15:52,106 --> 00:15:57,346
Substituting its entries yields equivalent
direct formulas.

208
00:15:57,546 --> 00:16:02,646
At a singular pose, choose bank zero and set
pitch to the signed pi over two.

209
00:16:02,646 --> 00:16:05,446
The source formula needs this boundary
correction.

210
00:16:05,646 --> 00:16:08,446
Clamping protects against small rounding
errors,

211
00:16:08,546 --> 00:16:11,966
rather than hiding non-unit inputs or
mismatched conventions.

212
00:16:12,725 --> 00:16:16,760
Read facing and bank in these three driving
excerpts.

213
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,640
Edits between them are not rotation
interpolation.

214
00:16:23,192 --> 00:16:26,272
Imagining three attached body axes helps

215
00:16:26,532 --> 00:16:28,672
compare poses separately from the camera
frame.

216
00:16:31,864 --> 00:16:35,444
Matrix/quaternion conversion must preserve the
axes relationship,

217
00:16:35,824 --> 00:16:41,916
without automatically explaining speed or
collision.

218
00:16:42,016 --> 00:16:45,556
With forward and up defined consistently, the
third axis is determined too.

219
00:16:45,556 --> 00:16:50,136
Consistent handedness and axis directions are
the starting point for conversion checks.

220
00:16:50,136 --> 00:16:57,348
Visible gameplay does not prove a particular
conversion implementation.

221
00:16:57,608 --> 00:17:02,308
It supplies the question; independent
calculations verify values and boundaries.

222
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:10,680
We will return to axis-angle and check a
midpoint and a conversion round trip.

223
00:17:11,300 --> 00:17:16,260
Axis-angle to quaternion uses the half-angle
formula: cosine for the scalar,

224
00:17:16,360 --> 00:17:19,720
unit axis times sine for the vector.

225
00:17:19,980 --> 00:17:22,600
For a short representative angle,

226
00:17:22,600 --> 00:17:25,880
validate unit length and flip all components
if w is negative.

227
00:17:26,120 --> 00:17:28,820
The angle is twice arccosine of clamped w.

228
00:17:29,040 --> 00:17:32,420
When the vector part is long enough, normalize
it to get the axis.

229
00:17:32,660 --> 00:17:34,640
At zero rotation the axis is undefined. Our
code selects x by convention,

230
00:17:34,840 --> 00:17:39,100
rather than claiming a unique physical axis.

231
00:17:39,100 --> 00:17:42,860
At180degrees, opposite axes give the same
endpoint orientation.

232
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:45,900
Manage path direction and accumulated turns
separately.

233
00:17:46,700 --> 00:17:52,160
First exercise: what is the halfway SLERP pose
from identity to z90degrees?

234
00:17:52,260 --> 00:17:58,340
The answer is45degrees, stored using cosine
and sine of the22.5-degree half-angle.

235
00:17:58,500 --> 00:18:02,540
Convert to a matrix and rotate[1,0,0].

236
00:18:02,540 --> 00:18:06,120
The result is[sqrt2/2,sqrt2/2,0]; check its
unit length.

237
00:18:06,120 --> 00:18:12,300
Second exercise: convert a z180-degree matrix
to a quaternion and back.

238
00:18:12,540 --> 00:18:19,620
The z branch gives[0,0,0,1]. The reconstructed
matrix has diagonal[-1,-1,1].

239
00:18:19,920 --> 00:18:24,240
An opposite quaternion sign can still pass
when the matrices agree.

240
00:18:24,440 --> 00:18:27,160
Compare entry errors and transformed basis
axes.

241
00:18:27,783 --> 00:18:32,523
We aligned signs, preserved unit length and
interpolated rotations along a short arc.

242
00:18:32,783 --> 00:18:37,043
Constant speed also needed fixed endpoints and
linear time;

243
00:18:37,283 --> 00:18:39,543
accumulated large turns require separate
records.

244
00:18:39,547 --> 00:18:42,423
Conversions preserve axes and multiplication
conventions

245
00:18:42,423 --> 00:18:46,143
and handle zero,180degrees and Euler
singularities.

246
00:18:46,343 --> 00:18:49,223
Go beyond memorizing representation names:
rotate a

247
00:18:49,223 --> 00:18:52,403
small vector and reverse the transformation to
verify it.

248
00:18:52,803 --> 00:18:54,823
This completes the3Drotation chapter.

249
00:18:54,823 --> 00:18:59,063
Next we will combine orientation and position
in rigid-body transformations.
